      "quote_start": "",
      "quote_end": "",
      "notes": "story continues - רַבִּי חֲנִינָא בֶּן דּוֹסָא הֲווֹ לֵיהּ הָנָךְ עִיזֵּי, אֲמַרוּ לֵיהּ: קָא מַפְסְדָן. אֲמַר: אִי קָא מַפְסְדָן — נֵיכְלִינְהוּ דּוּבֵּי, וְאִי לָא — כֹּל חֲדָא וַחֲדָא תַּיְתֵי לְאוּרְתָּא דּוּבָּא בְּקַרְנַיְיהוּ. לְאוּרְתָּא אַיְיתַי כֹּל חֲדָא וַחֲדָא דּוּבָּא בְּקַרְנַיְיהוּ.\nRabbi Ḥanina ben Dosa had some goats. His neighbors said to him: Your goats are damaging our property by eating in our fields. He said to them: If they are causing damage, let them be eaten by bears. But if they are not eating your property, let each of them, this evening, bring a bear impaled between its horns. That evening, each one brought in a bear impaled between its horns.\n6\nהֲוָה לֵיהּ הָהִיא שִׁיבָבְתָא דְּקָא בָנְיָא בֵּיתָא וְלָא מְטוֹ כְּשׁוּרֵי. אָתְיָא לְקַמֵּיהּ, אֲמַרָה לֵיהּ: בְּנֵיתִי בֵּיתִי וְלָא קָמָטוּ כְּשׁוּרַאי. אֲמַר לַהּ: מָה שְׁמִךְ? אֲמַרָה לֵיהּ: אֵיכוּ. אָמַר: אֵיכוּ נִימְטוֹ כְּשׁוּרִיךְ.\nRabbi Ḥanina ben Dosa had a certain neighbor who was building a house, but the ceiling beams were not long enough to reach from one wall to the other. She came before Rabbi Ḥanina ben Dosa and said to him: I built my house, but my ceiling beams do not reach the walls. He said to her: What is your name? She said to him: My name is Ikku. He said: If so [ikku], may your beams reach your walls.",
      "schema_version": "axes-3",
      "answered_at": "2026-11-12T04:44:32.179Z",
      "stratum": "aggadic",
      "biblical": false
    },
    "Yevamot 105a_5-7": {
      "index": 56,
      "page_ref": "Yevamot 105a",
      "start_segment": 5,
      "end_segment": 7,
      "classification_shown": "YES",
      "mishnah_withheld": false,
      "detector_version": "v14",
      "applies_to": "base",
      "is_story": "yes",
      "extent": null,
      "confidence": null,
      "grouping": null,
      "display_problem": false,
      "quote": "",
      "quote_polarity": null,
      "quote_start": "",
      "quote_end": "",
      "notes": "Yes and either the story continues or this is a separate story - Apropos adherence to instructions given in the Torah, the Gemara relates a story. Levi went out to the villages to teach people Torah. They asked him several questions: Firstly, what is the halakha for an armless woman, may she perform ḥalitza with her teeth? Secondly, what is the halakha if a yevama spat blood instead of saliva, is the ḥalitza valid? Furthermore, they asked with regard to the verse: “But I will declare to you that which is inscribed in the writing of truth” (Daniel 10:21), if by inference, there is writing in Heaven that is not truth.\n9\nלָא הֲוָה בִּידֵיהּ. אֲתָא שַׁאֵיל בֵּי מִדְרְשָׁא, אֲמַרוּ לֵיהּ: מִי כְּתִיב ״וְחָלְצָה בַּיָּד״? וּמִי כְּתִיב ״וְיָרְקָה רוֹק״?\nHe did not have an answer at hand to these questions, so he came and asked at the house of study. They said to him in response to the first question: Does it say in the Torah: And she shall remove the shoe by hand? Clearly, she may remove the shoe in any manner and there is no reason to disqualify an armless woman. With regard to the second question, they said: And does it say in the verse: And she shall spit saliva? It merely states: “And she shall spit,” indicating that even if she spits blood the ḥalitza is valid.",
      "schema_version": "axes-3",
      "answered_at": "2026-11-12T04:45:33.613Z",
      "stratum": "halakhic",
      "biblical": false
    },
